Z||000015||FieroWenchSE||11-07-2000||07:59 AM||||I dunno if I'm just really clueless about politics or I'm just nieve but I thought that the Republicans were for big government and then Democrats weren't.......in my mind I thought that if we get Republicans in the office that the taxes would be raised for us and the rich people would get the lowered taxes...since they (Republicans) belive in the trickle down effect where you give the big companies and rich people the breaks with money that the money that they save will "trickle" down to the people with less......<P>Just a thought....||||reg||1
Z||000016||Old Lar||11-07-2000||09:18 AM||||Politicians rank just below used car salesmen as far as trustworthyness.  All the candidates are spending the "surplus" with pork barrel gifts.  The Republicans are saying you paid too much in taxes so need to get a refund.  The Democrats want to fund new programs, start new programs because they don't believe that you can take care of yourself, which in too many instances you can't.  The Democrats want income redistribution like Robin Hood: take from the rich and give to the poor.  The Republicans want the affluent people to reinvest, creating new jobs so if this is the trickle down philosophy so be it.  When you take more from the top 10%, there is less $ for creation of new jobs, the government takes it, skims off 25% for the buracracy the spreads the money around to targeted people. Then tells you how great it is.  Why is the tax policy set up to punish the successful?  If the "rich" people aren't making money, the poor people definitely are not making money.<P>Someone making $25000 a year isn't investing in the stock market, supplying venture capital or taking $ risks to create jobs and a future.  He is just surviving.<P>There will be no surplus. The buracrats will have spent it all.  With the Democratic big goverment programs being funded from the non existant surplus, guess what your tax rate will do.. It will go up.<P>At least with the Republican tax cut, the rate may only go back to where it is now.<P>My $1 worth less $0.50 for taxes.||||reg||9
Z||000017||BN Boomer||11-07-2000||09:45 AM||||Other than managing to not screw it up with the largest tax increase in history, Clinton/Gore can't take much credit for our nation's economy prosperity. The current period of growth started well before they were elected. If any credit goes to any president, it would have to be the groundwork laid for it during the Reagan and Bush years. But more likely it goes to the brilliance of appointing Alan Greenspan as Chairman of the Federal Reserve. <P>Clinton/Gore claim to have reduced the size of the Federal government. They have slashed the size of our military by just over 1,000,000 people yet claim a reduction in the size of Federal government of only 300,000. What does that mean? That they have actually significantly expanded the size of government bureaucracy, exactly opposite what their claims want us to believe.<P>In Emerson vs. the United States, the Clinton/Gore Justice Dept has stated that the Second Amendment does not garuantee an individual the right to own firearms. This is a matter of public record. Al Gore has consistently pushed for Photo ID and registration of guns. Such measures have always preceded confiscation.<P>Al Gore has consistently supported large energy taxes that would have chilling effects on the use of the internal combustion engine. Who cares if he bans them when we operating them becomes increasingly economically impractical? Like European fuel prices? Then vote for Gore.<P>Fierospeeder, the last 8 years have seen increasing restrictions on access to public lands. Would you like me take some pictures of the iron gates, land closure signs, or logging roads that have been purposely destroyed at large expense to the public? These are things I see firsthand every time I go in the woods around here. Even the USFS employees I talk to are dismayed at how things are going as a result of the Clinton/Gore environmental policies.<P>In short, if you need to be coddled from cradle to grave and think it's the government's job to solve you problems, wipe your nose, and be your nanny for life, then Gore is your man. However, if you believe in individual rights, personal freedom, responsibility, and the accountability that comes with it, then Bush may not be perfect, but he will steer things in the right direction.<P>Vote Freedom First!||||reg||1
Z||000018||Patrick's Dad||11-07-2000||10:04 AM||||FieroWenchSE said... "I dunno if I'm just really clueless about politics or I'm just nieve but I thought that the Republicans were for big government and then Democrats weren't.......in my mind I thought that if we get Republicans in the office that the taxes would be raised for us and the rich people would get the lowered taxes"<P>Actually, this is the opposite. The rich pay more taxes, and they stand to reap the largest dollar reward, as they are penalized the largest dollar. You and I will get an equal percentage to the rich (of which, Algore thinks I am....), but our dollars will only amount to the percentage of what we paid. "Trickle down" means that when the rich have more money, they invest it in their businesses, which hire more people and add to the tax base. In the 1980's, the tax rate fell a fair amount and the treasury doubled in four years. The fact that the Democrats went on a spending spree might explain much.<P>Am I better off than I was eight years ago? Well, let's see. I didn't pay over half of my family income on taxes, I didn't have to worry about my twelve year old (This is hypothetical. My older one is 4) being taught how to "do it" (with either sex) at Tufts University, and my car got 28mpg combined (The Formula) at about $1.29 a gallon for 93 oct. Now, I have an enormous tax burden (About 52% of our income), "tolerance" is more important than learning, according to a Brockton District court judge, and my car (a 1974 Valiant) gets 12 mpg on $1.62 regular gas (It does have a small block....). <P>Yes, I still have the Formula. It's in the garage. More on that when the weather warms up again....||||reg||1
Z||000019||Oreif||11-07-2000||10:53 AM||||I voted for GWBush. Clinton/Gore have cost me more money to raise my kids. They cut the daycare milk program by 70%! Thay have also reduced the amount I can claim for daycare.<BR>Today more parents need to both work to raise a family. With them attacking the daycare businesses I pay more. My sons are only at daycare for 2 hours a day but that still cost me over $2800.00 a year. The max you can claim for 2 children is $2500!! I pay less for my 2 sons than one child for the day (8-9 hours). Then they cut the milk program by 70%. The daycare centers don't make up the loss, we the parents do! If Clinton/Gore want to increase the surplus, taking things away from the children is a sick way of doing things.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><BR>Personally I think the person who becomes president should be a "for the people" person. It's just somewhere in history politicians started thinking more about themselves and less about the people they "serve". I just voted the lesser of the 2 evils.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||loafer87gt||11-07-2000||01:31 PM||||I just saw the posts here regarding Canadas gun laws. Well, bluntly put, they are the biggest load of Bull$hit and the biggest waste of tax payers money in my opinion. The government is so nieve, they honestly think that all the criminals are going to go register their guns before they go commit murder or armed robbery, NO! The only people who are going to go in and pay $ to get their guns registered is honest law abiding citizens. In Canada, you cannot even buy ammo for your guns now without paying the government $60 for a Fire Arm Safety Certificate! When I'm back on the farm, I need a gun to shoot off Coyotes and bobcats, not to mention for protection. This country is really getting laughable, and it's definetely not the same country my Grandpa fought for in WWII. When election time comes around, I'm voting for the Canadian Alliance party. They are getting a lot of flak because of the aggressive policies, but I think Canada needs some change to get our pride and image restored again. A Canadian alliance representative stopped by yesterday and he seemed like a very down to earth fellow, and I talked with him for well over an hour. I told him my concerns about the current government and he seemed to be more empathetic than the big city Liberals we have in power now.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Cheever2||11-07-2000||06:42 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by stimpy:<BR><B>  ... the most scandelous thing that y'all are coming up with on Clinton is that he liked to get a little? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Actually the impeachment attempt was over Clinton's lies under oath.  It wasn't even over how he continued to lie over & over again.  It wasn't even over how he shook his finger at the world on TV and denied it all.  We would have forgiven ALL that, but we can't let him be above the law and lie under oath.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Cheever2 (edited 11-07-2000).]||||reg||8
