Z||000024||Songman||11-10-2000||01:18 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by stimpy:<BR><B>  According to you, Republicans credit for everything good, Democrats the blame for everything bad.  It's so nice when everything is painted in black and white. Let's see, Iran-Contra... must have been that Democratically controlled congress that [b]made</B> the Reagan adnistration commit high treason. <BR>The most scandelous thing that y'all are coming up with on Clinton is that he liked to get a little? Millions of dollars spent trying to impeach a President that we re-elected <B>knowing</B> that he fooled around? I guess that is a burden to be shared by every Democrat too.  Open up <B>your</B> eyes. Cause it's obvious that you don't get it either. [/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The people who need to open their eyes are the ones that think that any gov't can be ran without some covert activity, and that the public has a right to know all of that activity. Personally, I think Ollie North should have been given a medal instead of treated like a criminal. And at least Reagan had the balls to stand up to little third world countries instead of trying to make deals with them at our expense.<P>One more thing that comes directly off of this topic, in saying that the public doesn't have to know everything... remember that little thing called the Gulf War when there were cameras showing everything as we went along. Well, that was a fun little skirmish and it made all of us at home feel involved. What if that had been in a real war like VietNam or Korea and the media had been giving away troop positions and causing out brothers and fathers to be killed. We wouldn't have liked it quite so much then would we? And yes, there was some media in VietNam, but not to the same extent. Back in the days of a strong America, they would not have been allowed to show some of the things they showed during the Gulf War. But of course, back then you couldn't burn an American Flag either... This goes back to my original point of this thread... there are cases where the people of the US only need to know the result and not the action that got the result.<P>I'm sure some people won't understand my meaning, and I probably offended some Gulf War veterans. That was not my intent. I am a US Army vet myself. I am just saying that these were very different times with very different circumstances. And while I am offending people, it still bothers me that vets of the Gulf War came home as heroes while VietNam vets still can't even walk down the streets with their heads up and be proud for fighting for their country (in the eyes of a lot of the population and the gov't anyway). I'm not even saying whether the war was right or wrong, just that these men were all called by their country and they did their duty and they deserve better.<P>As someone said in another message,, end of rant.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000025||2M4 Dale||11-10-2000||03:32 AM||||The nice neat little war in the Persian Gulf was fought against the 5th largest military power on the planet.......I would say that we just kicked thier butts........It helps when the political engine behind the troops has the intestinal fortitude to open up a big can of whoop-ass on an enemy....Unlike the Johnson administration in the 60's...the Bush people didn't pansy around !!!!!!!!!!<P>We won the Viet Nam War at least twice and the government pulled the troops back each time...Whats up with that ???||||reg||8
Z||000026||stimpy||11-10-2000||10:43 AM||||Give North a medal for what???  What exactly was a leftist Nicarauguan government doing to negatively impact our life in the United States, other then possibly interfering with the flow of cocaine into the United States? Even that was fixed by utilising military transports. Don't get me started on the conspiracy theory boo-yah!||||reg||1
Z||000027||BAM-BAM||11-10-2000||08:10 PM||||  The vietnam war was a political cluster F#$^. The Government sacrificed the lives of over 65,000 american soldiers for their own financial and political gain. Vietnam vets are true HEROS. They went where there weren't wanted, fought for a cause they didn't believe in, and many died for nothing.But all in all, they answered the call of a corrupt government that could care less about them. The persian gulf was a completely different story  and you can thank Vietnem for that. The people weren't about to get caught up in the same type of political slaughter house that was Vietnam. The media was there for inverse political reasons. It was politicaly correct to show the world (this time) how powerful we really are. As for the people that called the Vietnam Vet's "Baby killer" "murderer", for the people that spit on them in the airports and bus stations. You should have been the ones on the front line every time. Those soldiers had no choice, They were sent to die by the people we elected. Those people (politicians) should have been the ones scorned and humiliated and left suicidal by a nonundestanding uniformed and ignorant nation.<P>  Here's a little statement that a wise man once told me and everyone on gods earth should head this: "Dont ask any more of others than you are willing to give of yourself". To often we want others to do our bidding for us when we are to afraid to do it ourselves. <P>  End Of Rant!!!!!||||reg||8
